Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — source-developed working scene
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-10-10
Status: Independent screenplay development under DEC-0019; refinements during production

INT. GUESTHOUSE OUTSIDE HEREFORD - WEDNESDAY MORNING

Zoe sits at the archive laptop. The original drive stays in her bag; the protected working copy supplies RCP-0193.

ZOE
No letters. No photograph. Only what explains the decision.

Elias waits for her to choose the next field.

BIOLOGICAL CHILD — DECEASED. Age four. Helen's later pregnancy unsafe. Counselling. A lawful adoption attempt deferred over replacement concerns.

ZOE
They'd lost a daughter.

ELIAS
Yes.

Zoe opens the sponsor summary. AC019. Pre-existing relationship with the male applicant.

ZOE
Mac already knew Brian.

Withdrawal: Brian's legality objection. Four days after sponsor contact, Helen reopened the route. The promised stable, loving home would outweigh the barrier to ordinary adoption.

The disclosures are separate: existing safeguarding pathway, origin process potentially unfinished, changed records, continuing restricted administration. Both applicants accepted.

ZOE
They knew there was a line.

ELIAS
Enough to see it. Not proof they knew every altered order.

The suitability fields describe an ordinary street, work, routines, attachments. Zoe almost smiles at a memory of Brian defending a broken bathroom cabinet.

Then: FAMILIAL RESEMBLANCE DESIRED. PRIOR-CHILD ANCHOR ACTIVE.

A minimum definition: hair, face shape, crooked smile. Further detail remains folded shut.

ZOE
No more.

Elias closes it immediately.

A17-04221. MATCH SCORE 97.

Zoe turns away from the screen.

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S BEDROOM - SAME MORNING

Helen brings a storage box down from the loft. Brian joins her on the floor.

A photograph: their daughter laughing by a climbing frame. One small shoe lies beside it.

HELEN
She looked like her.

BRIAN
Mac didn't say it meant anything.

HELEN
He didn't have to.

Helen puts the photograph back. Not hidden under the other objects. Their daughter is still dead. Zoe is still somebody else.

EXT. FOOTPATH - AFTERNOON

Zoe kicks a stone ahead of them.

ZOE
Helen loved me because I looked like her.

ELIAS
It may explain the first feeling. It doesn't explain five years.

ZOE
That sounds like defending her.

ELIAS
It doesn't give her the right.

They walk without agreeing to make either truth smaller.

ZOE
Something horrible started something real. Then the real thing hid the horrible part.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
Disgusting.

ELIAS
Yes.

ZOE
Normal agreement. Rare.

INT. GUESTHOUSE - EVENING

Zoe reads only the attachment prediction. Love made Helen and Brian vulnerable to exposure; Zoe's attachment would make questioning the beginning painful too.

She closes the household profile before P3 — CONTROLLED VISUAL INTRODUCTION.

ZOE
How did they first see me?

ELIAS
We haven't opened that.

Her paperback lies beside the computer. She takes the book, leaves the file closed for tonight.
